  "notes": "The third pillar of the Frankfurt production axis, named in the research alongside Snap! as an originator of the idiom. Two entry-specific facts move the numbers off the canonical grammar. The kick is \"heavier, more NRG-oriented… reflecting the post-1992 tempo escalation toward 150 BPM documented for the idiom\", so the band runs to 140, the kick is allowed a fifth hit, and the accent tier is the loudest in the 1990s block at 116-127. And the harmony carries \"a dramatic diminished or chromatic passing chord into the chorus\", which is `susOrDimProb` at 0.22 rather than an invented numeral — the research states the device, not a degree, and a numeral names a degree only. Everything else is the cited teardown: kick 1/5/9/13, open hats 3/7/11/15, clap 5/13. The melodic material is the documented male-rap / female-sung split \"with the sung chorus carrying the melodic hook and the rap carrying the rhythm\", over hoover stabs and orchestral-sample stabs. ⚠ The research marks the key, drum and tempo specifics as genre-derived and the diminished-chord claim `[LOW-CONF]`; both marks are repeated here. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are 0.22, the velocity tiers and every density.",
